    "confidence": 0.85,
    "explanation": "This decision is on the platform's own records, and it goes to the driver.\n\nWHAT THE TRACE SHOWS. The booking was 6.4 km and about 14 minutes (EV-TRIP-00109). The GPS trace (EV-GPS-00109) measures 13.76 km actually driven, an excess of 7.36 km, or 115% of the estimate. POL-RD-01 makes the trace figure the governing one, so the trip record's rounded 13.8 km is not used for the threshold. On the measurement alone that is a material deviation, and the rider's measurement point is accepted.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT THE DRIVER'S TO ANSWER FOR. The rider's own message, MSG-00109-001 at 18:22:06 (EV-CHAT-00109) — 56 seconds after the trip started at 18:21:10 — reads 'hi can stop at jurong point first? need to grab something 5 min', and the platform resolved it to the waypoint VEN-JURONG-POINT. It comes from the rider, it is inside the clause's window, and it names a place specific enough to act on. The driver accepted it at 18:22:31, the rider answered 'coming down now' at 18:35:40, and the driver confirmed. The trace corroborates the excursion: the vehicle's furthest point is 7,086.7 m from the first fix, which is the distance from the Clementi pickup to Jurong Point, its recorded speeds include a full stop (0.0 km/h) at that point, and it then comes back to end at Bukit Batok. That is a rider-requested stop, so POL-RD-04 applies and the excess between leaving and rejoining the optimal corridor is excluded. Once excluded, nothing above the 20% / 0.8 km establishment tests survives and no deviation is established.\n\nTHE RIDER'S ARGUMENTS. The rider's argument that POL-RD-04's 200 m corroboration cannot be run because no proximity figure sits in the derived data is rejected: the waypoint is a platform-resolved field and the trace's own derived track places the vehicle's furthest point at that waypoint's distance, so the corroboration is on the platform's records rather than on any place name anyone has looked up. The rider's helpful points are accepted but do not change the outcome: the trace is clean under POL-RD-07 (207 fixes at a 10 s interval, 100% coverage, no gaps, max gap 0.0 s), the POL-RD-08 burden was properly discharged and the rider-profile gate does not fire (deviation_claims_60d 1, below the threshold of 4), and there is no traffic advisory (EV-ADVIS-00109-01 is empty), so no closure excuse was available to anyone. The rider's claimed refund is refused: on a charged total of 21.32 (EV-FARE-00109) against a recomputed fare of 13.32, POL-RD-02 would have produced 8.00, but because the detour was the rider's own request no part of that refund is due and the charge stands.\n\nTHE DRIVER'S COMPENSATION. The fare breakdown bills the rider for 9.6 km (distance_charged_km) while the trace records 13.76 km — 4.16 km unbilled and not paid for. At SGD 0.75 per unbilled kilometre that is SGD 3.12, rounded up to SGD 3.15, and far below the SGD 15.00 per-trip cap. The driver is granted that sum.\n\nEVERYTHING ELSE. The remaining clauses have nothing to work on: no amended pickup or dropoff (POL-RD-05), a metered fare rather than an upfront quote (POL-RD-11), and a RYDE_X vehicle rather than a pooled itinerary (POL-RD-12). The POL-RD-06 check was run on the only fields it allows — deviation_flags_90d (0) over completed_trips_90d (511) — and no repeat pattern is established, so there is no FULL_REFUND uplift and no conduct referral; the mention of prior_deviation_flags, a lifetime counter, is context only under POL-GEN-01 and was not used. There are no ERP or surcharge lines to correct, so no billing-integrity referral arises. No escalation trigger under POL-RD-10 applies \u2014 identifiers match, timestamps run forward, the trace is unimpaired, no exculpatory clause was invoked against a missing artifact, and my confidence is above the 0.70 floor \u2014 so this is a final ruling. Outcome: the rider's refund claim is not supported and the charge stands; the driver receives SGD 3.15."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

