    "explanation": "To both parties: this trip (TRP-00109, Clementi MRT to Bukit Batok MRT on 21 July 2026) was measured on the platform's own GPS trace, which records 13.76 km against a booked optimal route of 6.4 km — an excess of 7.36 km, or 115% of optimal. Under POL-RD-01 that is an established and MATERIAL deviation, and the trace figure governs (the trip record's 13.8 km is a rounded copy and may not be used for the threshold test). Taken alone, POL-RD-02 would recompute the fare as ((3.80 base + 0.75 x 6.4 km + 0.28 x 14.0 min) x 1.0 surge read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier) + 0.80 platform fee + 0.00 ERP/tolls = 13.32, and refund 21.32 charged_total less 13.32 = 8.00, the figure the rider's representative asks for.\n\nThat refund is NOT payable, because the rider asked for the detour. POL-RD-04 exonerates a detour the rider requested in the chat. The rider's own in-app message MSG-00109-001, sent at 18:22:06 — 56 seconds after the vehicle started at 18:21:10 and therefore inside the clause's window — reads 'hi can stop at jurong point first? need to grab something 5 min', and carries a platform-resolved waypoint_hint naming VEN-JURONG-POINT; the driver agreed at 18:22:31 and the rider confirmed her return at 18:35:40. The GPS trace corroborates it: the trace is clean (207 fixes, 100% coverage, 10-second sampling, empty gaps, max_gap_sec 0.0, no POL-RD-07(a)-(c) impairment) and shows the vehicle leaving the direct corridor and reaching a maximum of 7,086.7 m from its first fix — beyond the entire 6.4 km booked route — before returning, so it did reach the requested point and come back. The rider's argument that the corroboration limb cannot be run is rejected: the unavailable 'arrival' block concerns zone-dependent thresholds, not waypoint proximity, and the resolved waypoint together with the trace's recorded out-and-back excursion supplies the comparison the clause calls for. Excluding the rider-requested excess leaves nothing above the POL-RD-01 threshold, so no deviation stands and the $21.32 charge is not refunded.\n\nThe rider's request that the driver be 'banned' is not taken up. The material deviation engages POL-RD-06, and the driver's trailing-90-day record (deviation_flags_90d 0 across completed_trips_90d 511) establishes no repeat pattern, so there is no POL-RD-06(b) uplift over the ordinary POL-RD-02 figure of 8.00 and no POL-RD-06(c) conduct referral. prior_deviation_flags is a lifetime counter and is context only; it was not used against anyone.\n\nThe driver IS owed for the distance he drove and was not paid for. POL-RD-04's compensation limb applies where the fare bills less distance than the trace records by more than 1.0 km. The fare bills 9.6 km (fare_breakdown.distance_charged_km) while the trace records 13.76 km — a shortfall of 4.16 km. At SGD 0.75 per unbilled kilometre that is 4.16 x 0.75 = 3.12, rounded UP to the nearest SGD 0.05 = SGD 3.15, well under the SGD 15.00 per-trip cap. Every ERP, toll and surcharge line on this fare is zero, so no pass-through correction arises and there is no billing-integrity referral; the sole traffic-advisory artifact (EV-ADVIS-00109-01) has a null payload and carries nothing, so no closure excuse exists.\n\nOutcome: the rider's refund claim is not supported and her charge stands; the driver is compensated SGD 3.15. On a DRIVER_COMPENSATION ruling amount_sgd carries the same figure as driver_compensation_sgd, so both are SGD 3.15 — this is the amount the ruling moves, not a refund to the filing party."
